BECOME A COACH

Help Others Develop in Obstacle Sport

The Obstacle Course Racing Association of Ireland (OCRA ÉIREANN) is developing a structured coaching pathway for people who want to support athletes at every level of obstacle sport.

Coaches play an important role in helping people train safely, develop obstacle skills, improve performance and remain involved in the sport over the long term.


Who Can Become an OCR Coach?

You do not need to be an elite athlete to become a coach.

Good coaching requires much more than being able to complete difficult obstacles.

Effective coaches need to develop skills in areas such as:

- Communication
- Athlete welfare
- Session planning
- Technical instruction
- Safety
- Motivation
- Observation
- Feedback
- Athlete development

People may enter OCR coaching from many different backgrounds.


The OCRA Coaching Pathway

The OCRA Coaching Programme provides a progressive pathway from introductory coaching through to national and international performance environments.

The pathway includes:

1. OCR Coach — Foundations
2. OCR Coach — Participation
3. OCR Coach — Performance
4. OCR Coach — National
5. OCR Coach — International
6. OCR Coach — Senior International
7. OCR Coach — Master Coach Trainer


Starting Your Coaching Journey

The early stages of the pathway focus on developing the foundations needed to coach responsibly.

This includes areas such as:

- Safeguarding
- Athlete welfare
- Health and safety
- Inclusive coaching
- Ethical coaching
- Basic obstacle technique
- Session planning
- Coaching communication


Where Can Coaching Lead?

OCR coaches may work with:

- Beginners
- Recreational athletes
- Clubs
- Youth athletes
- Competitive athletes
- Age Group athletes
- Elite athletes
- Adaptive athletes
- National-level competitors
- International athletes

Experienced coaches may also progress towards mentoring and developing other coaches.


Existing Experience

People entering OCR coaching may already have relevant experience or qualifications from areas such as:

- Athletics
- Strength and conditioning
- Personal training
- Climbing
- Gymnastics
- Ninja sport
- Parkour
- Functional fitness
- Endurance sport

OCRA may recognise relevant previous education and practical experience where appropriate.

Obstacle sport-specific requirements must still be met.


